1:player
2:Argh...
3:Nana Shiraishi
4:Why haven't you played a tile yet? Are you feeling troubled because you drew something good and can't decide what to keep?
5:...I think I see little figures dancing on the table.
6:Umm... Did you eat any mushrooms before we started playing...?
7:No, I'm just feeling a little muddle-headed because I didn't get enough sleep last night.
8:As I look at the Half Flush consisting of Man tiles in my hand, my tired mind reels with confusion, and I can't decide which tile to play.
9:(whisper) If I want to give myself as many options as possible later... should I play this one?
10:???
11:Nana-senpai, are you there?!
12:The loud shout behind me makes me startle with surprise, and the tile in my hand clatters as it drops onto the table. I instantly realize that I have played the wrong tile when I am shocked to my senses.
13:Ah, forget it. I can't take back the tile anyway, so I'll pretend nothing happened...
14:So this is where you are, Nana-senpai!
15:Ai, what brings you here? Junior, this is one of my juniors at school.
16:Hi.
17:Ai
18:So you're that famous "junior" Nana always tells us about. Let's get along with each other.
19:Ai grabs my hand and shakes it up and down vigorously with such enthusiasm that my arm starts to hurt.
20:Did you come here to ask me for some help?
21:You're as astute as ever, but I'm not in a hurry. We can chat after you finish your game. I'll sit here on the side.
22:...She's quite similar to you.
23:Hehe, do you mean she's as passionate as me?
24:Well...
25:Comment tactfully
26:Comment honestly
27:She really is quite enthusiastic, and she can hit it off right away with anyone, just like you.
28:I get the feeling that you're making a dig at me. Whatever, please take that as a compliment.
29:But even if you are praising me, I'll still take your tile. Ron! It's a Pinfu worth one Han.
30:Just like that, my turn as the dealer was ended by an insignificant one-Han hand. But judging from Nana Shiraishi's hand and taking into account that this is only the fourth turn, she could have ignored my 3 Man and waited to complete a Mixed Triple Sequence with a 4, 5, and 6 to assemble a better hand.
31:It might actually be a good thing to have my turn as the dealer cut short safely by dealing into a low-Han hand. Thank you for sparing me.
32:Hehe, I'm glad you understand.
33:She's reckless like you.
34:Reckless?! You're starting to sound less and less cute.
35:In that case, I won't win this game with your 3 Man because that would be too RECKLESS, right?
36:Oh? You're not taking my tile to win the game even though I just made a dig at you? Thank you for sparing me.
38:Several turns later
39:I thanked her too early... So she didn't call Ron with my 3 Man because she's been waiting for a Mixed Triple Sequence with a 4, 5, and 6...?
41:It hurts so badly to lose by a Haneman as the dealer...
42:After the Hanchan game ends, the dojo owner stops me and Nana Shiraishi apologetically right after we leave our seats.
43:Dojo Owner
44:Excuse me, the table over there is missing a mahjong partner. I was going to play, but something happened at the last minute, and I need to go out for a while.
45:Can one of you watch the cashier for me while the other person takes my place at the table? There are no other regulars here right now. Please help me.
46:Sure, in that case... Nana, you can watch the cashier and chat with your junior while I go play mahjong.
47:Are you sure you'll be okay? You were yawning the whole time while drawing tiles in the fourth South round.
48:I think I'll be fine... I'll just behave myself and play defensively until she returns. I'll consider it to be my victory if I don't deal into someone's big hand.
49:Doesn't Ai have something to discuss with you? It won't be good to keep her waiting for too long.
50:Nana, why don't you play? Your friend can barely keep their eyes open. I can wait a little longer.
51:Nana Shiraishi pats my shoulder after hearing Ai's words.
52:Since Ai said that, you should go get some rest. I'm sorry, Ai, just wait a little longer for me.
53:Thank you so much. I'll come back as soon as possible.
54:After the proprietress leaves, I sit down in front of the cashier and chat perfunctorily with Ai to prevent myself from dozing off.
55:Ai tells me that Nana Shiraishi has been taking care of her since she was in junior high school, and that Nana Shiraishi was just as warm-hearted back then as she is now.
56:She helps everyone and is trusted by everyone. Don't you think she's really cool?!
57:Um, er...
58:I want to be as cool as her! That's why I took the entrance test to go to Asaha High School... I-I'm sorry, I think I've gotten a little too excited.
59:That's alright. It does sound pretty cool to be someone who is needed by others.
60:I knew we would be on the same channel since we're both Nana-senpai's juniors! Junior... oh wait, only senpai calls you that. What should I call you?
61:{var:player_nickname}.
62:{var:player_nickname}, if you're worried that you'll doze off, why don't we play a friendly match of mahjong on our mobile phones?
63:Haha... if I wanted to play mahjong for that purpose, I should have just gone to the mahjong table earlier.
64:Why don't I watch you play ranked matches instead? Think of it as a "What to discard?" session, and it'll also wake me up.
65:Considering that we haven't managed to find something interesting to talk about yet, this proposal is far more appropriate than awkward small talk. Without saying much, Ai opens the game and starts matching with opponents.
66:...Huh? Instead of checking out her hand after starting the match, she is looking at the information of her opponents one by one.
67:Are you confirming the playing style of your opponents?
68:Not really. It's not like I'll be able to do anything with that information even if I know their playing style. I just want to check how many points they have... Aha, this person's rank point is looking dangerous...
69:Their points... Someone close to dropping a tier might start to play more aggressively. Maybe she is devising some strategy based on this.
71:One hour later
72:No problem. The cashier's all yours.
73:Haha, I'll waive your playing fee next time.
74:Thanks! Ai...
75:I wanted to tell Ai to come watch Nana Shiraishi's game with me, but I decided against it because I could see that her game was also about to end.
76:Ai is currently leading by 13,000 points over the second-place player. Her opponent who is in fourth place just called Riichi. As a non-dealer player, she just needs to focus on defense to secure the top position.
77:However, the moment I look away from the screen, Ai seems to have played a risky tile for some reason! After the gorgeous winning animation plays on the screen, the opponent reveals their hand - apparently, Ai dealt into their Baiman.
78:Oh... I made a mistake. I was going to play the tile next to it.
79:What a shame. She probably wouldn't have made a mistake if she broke up her North Triplets. Maybe she was worried that her other opponents would attack, so she wanted to get rid of the tiles she played in the previous turns.
80:It's a pity, but now the opponent won't drop a tier, so this counts as my good deed for the day.
81:I'm back. My dear juniors, are you two getting along well?
82:Yeah, we are. Enjoy your chat. I'll be on my way now.
83:Wait, can you stay and listen to what I have to say too?
84:Me?
85:It's like this. A nearby mall will be holding a recreational mahjong competition during the weekend, and my classmates and I are thinking of participating in it as a team, but we're missing two people.
86:Ah, I understand now. You want me to help out and bring junior here with me along the way, right?
87:What? Do I have to go too?
88:Exactly! Nana-senpai tells me that you're always at mahjong dojos, so you must be good at playing mahjong, right?
89:Whether I'm good at mahjong or not is one thing. Why do you two make it sound like I'm an unemployed hobo who just plays mahjong all day long...
90:...Isn't that true though?
91:Are you asking me that seriously?
92:Suddenly, I find myself missing the title Kutsuji gave me. What was it again? "The famous guardian of Mahjong Soul Shrine's priestess, the best friend of students of Asaha High School, a core non-staff member of Soul..." Well, it's a little long, so I'll leave out the rest.
93:Don't mind the details. Well, are you free on the weekend? Playing mahjong with your senpai is a great opportunity to celebrate youth.
94:Thank you for not agreeing directly on my behalf, or else it'll really look like I always have nothing to do. But I am free on both days this weekend, so can you tell me the time and place?
95:After telling me the time and place where we'll be meeting up, Ai leaves the mahjong dojo in a hurry to tell her classmates the good news that they now have enough members.
96:How she can't stay still for even a second is really similar to you.
97:Haha, it's a good thing to be energetic! I'll see you later then. Remember to rest properly so you won't be muddle-headed like today.
98:You don't need to worry about me. I'll see you on the competition day.
99:Nana Shiraishi still doesn't understand me well enough. I always try my best in mahjong competitions.
100:The sky is blue with no clouds in sight on the day of the competition. My only complaint is that it just rained last night, soaking all the shared bicycles in front of my house. At least it's still early, which means I should be able to get to the mall in time even if I walk there.

102:By the time I arrive leisurely at the agreed place, I see that Nana Shiraishi is already waiting for me there.
103:Morning! You're early today.
104:Morning. You still arrived before me though.
105:Hehe, it's way too early for you to arrive earlier than me.
106:Stop! Is that a play on the word "early"? By the way, did you walk here or run here?
107:I ran here. The air is so fresh that it's a shame to not move around.
108:That's what I thought too. In that case, I didn't lose to you since I walked here.
109:Feeling competitive, are we? Well, that's a good mentality for the competition. Yes, this is very energetic of you!
110:Come on. It's a good day today. Let's win the competition!
111:You're that confident?
112:Hehe, I passed by Miss Kaavi's fortune-telling stall today, and she said my luck is unstoppable today and everything will go smoothly for me.
113:Er... she actually said something nice. If she is telling my fortune, she'll probably say that a calamity involving women is in store for me, and then she'll try to sell me a suspicious amulet.
114:A suspicious amulet? Doesn't Miss Kaavi always say that destiny can't be changed? Why would she sell you that type of thing?
115:She said it's a new business she started last month and offered me her "destiny support" services because I'm her regular customer. Didn't she give you the same offer?
116:Nope, at least she didn't make any such offer the last seven times she told my fortune.
117:...So she's only trying to scam me, huh?!
118:It's almost time. Let's go in so Ai and the others don't wait too long.
119:What? They're already here?
120:Yes.
121:It isn't often that I leave home so early. Why must I lose so thoroughly...?
123:It doesn't take long before the competition progresses to the semi-final lieutenant match of the top eight teams. Nana Shiraishi, our team captain, has gone out with one of our team members to buy bento boxes before the next match. Meanwhile, I, the vice-captain, am watching a game live on the TV on the wall with another team member in the lounge.
124:I start frowning as I watch...
125:Little Boy
126:I win!
127:On the screen, the little boy who moves after Ai takes the scoring sticks from Ai cheerfully. This is already the third time she has dealt into his hand.
128:She believed her Suji was safe and dealt in the first time, and she dealt into an Ippatsu the second time when calling chase Riichi. Just now, she played a risky tile even though she could play a safe tile.
129:Originally, the little boy's team was coming last, but they've now climbed back up to third place. It's only the first south round, so it's possible that they might even manage to climb up to second place by the end of this Hanchan...
130:After checking the scores of each team, I originally thought Ai was making mistakes consecutively because she had gotten complacent after knowing we were leading in the total points.
131:But when I saw her deliberately let go of the boy's deal-in while defending properly against the Riichi calls of the other two players, her actions gave me an unpleasant thought... Could Ai be playing unfairly?
132:Maybe I'm thinking too much about it
133:Ask others about what they think
134:...It's too early to come to that conclusion.
135:The team that's currently in second place at our table are members of the St. Tina's Girls' School Mahjong Club, and Ai seems to be deliberately trying to lower their points. If Ai wants to knock out a more troublesome opponent in advance and let a weaker team advance at the same time... I guess that's a reasonable tactic to win the competition, right?
136:What conclusion?
137:Oh... Nana, when did you get back?
138:I just got back and happened to see you frowning while deep in thought. What's up?
139:...I'm thinking about where we should go to have a good meal if we win today.
140:It might not be good to count your chickens before they hatch.
141:Didn't you tell me that our luck is unstoppable today...?
142:Forget it. I haven't known Ai for that long, and it isn't polite to make malicious guesses about others.
143:On this day, our luck manages to carry us until the very end, and we win the competition.
144:After the competition, Ai and her classmates want to go shopping, so Nana Shiraishi and I decide to go to Éternité to try a new coffee blend.
147:Hmm? Isn't that...
148:Before leaving the mall, I see our semi-final's opponent standing next to a fountain nearby. The girl wiping her tears among them is the Jyanshi who moved before Ai, the player Ai was targeting earlier.
149:Although I didn't play against her, I subconsciously avert my gaze from her.
150:...Although this is a recreational competition, the feeling of losing isn't nice. It's normal to feel uncomfortable when you see other people crying at times like this.
151:But we won fairly, so you don't need to pretend that you didn't see her. Isn't it the responsibility of the winner to face up to the tears of the loser?
152:Fairly...
153:After hearing Nana Shiraishi say this, I recall my earlier suspicion about Ai's unfair playing, which gives me mixed feelings...
154:Oh, you're frowning again. What are you thinking about?
155:Don't tell me you're thinking about dinner again this time. The same trick won't work on me twice!
156:I'll tell you then, but it's just a guess.
157:I tell Nana Shiraishi about Ai's strange behavior in the semi-finals. Of course, I also remind her there's a possible tactical reason for her actions.
158:After a moment of silence, Nana Shiraishi says "I understand" and ends the topic, then we go to the café as planned...
159:Mmm, the new blend is nice. The intense caffeine hit gives me a sleepless night and... the feeling of being listless on the next day.
160:I accidentally encounter Ai while shopping a few days later and raise my hand to greet her. However, she makes an unhappy expression when she sees me.
161:No one asked you to meddle in my affairs...
162:She says this when we walk past each other. A little concerned, I call Nana Shiraishi, who tells me she had asked Ai whether she wasn't playing fairly after watching the recording of the match. Apparently they had a dispute over this.
163:So she really did go easy on the little boy. Although it's not right for her to do something like that... I'm still sorry about making things unpleasant between you two.
164:Nonsense. Whatever her reason, she should play matches fairly, so you don't need to apologize to me.
165:But if you feel bad about it, I don't mind if you treat me to dinner to make up for it.
166:We'll do that. Where are y...
167:Before I can finish my sentence, a finger pokes my face stealthily.
168:I turn around and see Nana Shiraishi smiling at me.
169:It's not right for me to make such a judgment since I haven't known Ai for very long. I should ask someone who knows her better first.
170:Excuse me, can you tell me whether Ai has the habit of managing the number of points her opponents have in a mahjong game?
171:Member A
172:Managing the number of points others have? Hahaha, that's impossible. She isn't that skilled.
173:She's usually quite careless. Sometimes, she inexplicably deals into her opponent's good hand at the last minute when she's leading and playing well, just like what she did earlier. It shouldn't happen no matter how you look at it.
174:At the last minute...
175:Her words remind me of the mistake Ai made at the end of the fourth South round last time... Was that really a coincidence?
176:I'm back! Hmm? What's with that big frown on your face?
177:I'll investigate it myself
178:Discuss this matter with Nana Shiraishi
179:...Ai made a mistake just now, so I'm thinking that we'll need to get as many points as we can later.
180:A mistake? Oh, you're right. She dealt into her opponents' hands a few times...
181:I'll pretend nothing has happened until I find out what's going on. After this Hanchan ends, I'll find a reason to go outside and ask Ai about this before she returns to the lounge.
182:I'll apologize if it's just a misunderstanding. That will be better than mulling over it in my mind and letting it affect my performance in the next game.
183:...There they are.
184:Soon after I walk outside, I can see Ai and the little boy who moved after her in the game walking towards me, talking and laughing the whole time.
185:You're really amazing! Thank you for giving me so many points. I need to show off to the others later! But there are still two more matches to go. Will we really make it to the finals?
186:Don't worry about that. I'll find a way to help you advance so you can get the set of toys you want.
187:...An ugly scene plays out in front of me. I now have conclusive evidence that Ai has been going easy on the little boy.
188:Ai notices me looking at them after she says goodbye to the little boy and waves at me with a big smile on her face.
189:{var:player_nickname}, did you come here to get me? There's something I need to discuss with you.
190:If you want me to go easy on our opponents, then I refuse.
191:Ai's smile becomes awkward, probably because she can sense something wrong with my expression.
192:Ha-Haha... Hear me out first. It wasn't easy for those kids to make it to the top eight, and they just want the toy that's awarded as the prize. We're way ahead of them in terms of points, so I'm just helping them advance by dealing in a few times.
193:It's difficult for everyone to make it to the top eight. They should use their own strength to advance.
194:Don't be so serious. It's just a recreational competition that the mall has organized for advertising purposes. The staff will also be troubled if these children cry after missing out on the prize, right?
195:A recreational match is still a match, and I take all matches seriously. I won't help you do this.
196:Tsk... You're such a cheapskate. We're leading by a lot anyway, so what's wrong with helping those who are weaker than us?
197:I'm happy to be an honest cheapskate, and if receiving someone's help means that they will arbitrarily label me as a weakling, I wouldn't want them to help me. That's what I think anyway.
198:I'm an outsider, which is why you probably haven't mentioned this to me, but have you discussed this with your other teammates, especially your Nana-senpai whom you admire, in advance?
199:You don't need to worry about that. They'll definitely agree to help me. Someone as warm-hearted as Nana-senpai will definitely agree with what I'm doing.
200:So that means you haven't mentioned it to them...
201:Just give it a rest! Nana-senpai and I have been friends since junior high school, so I don't need you questioning our rapport! She'll definitely agree with me!
202:Oh? This sounds like a lively discussion. What are you talking about?
203:Oh, you're here, Nana-senpai.
204:I've come outside to get you two because you're taking so long. Go and eat something now. It'll be a pity if the hot bento box gets cold.
205:Ai and I stop arguing when we see Nana Shiraishi walk towards us, then Ai returns to the lounge first calmly. After watching her leave, Nana Shiraishi pats me on the back.
206:You're skilled enough to get those points back easily, so just forget about the mistakes Ai made earlier.
207:I don't know about that. I could have terrible luck later on.
208:It's not cute to say depressing things at this time, so listen up! You can do it if I say you can do it! Let's go and have a good meal before you dominate everyone at the mahjong table.
209:Since Nana Shiraishi mistakenly thinks that I've come to talk to Ai because of the deal-ins, I'll let her think that for now because I don't want to talk about people behind their backs.
210:...But the main reason I don't want to say anything is because I'm not confident about whether Nana Shiraishi agrees with Ai or not. Like Ai said, she has known Nana Shiraishi for a longer time than me...
212:Vice-captain battle
213:Next Player
214:Good! I got it! With six Ura Doras, that's a total of...
215:Previous Player
216:You can stop counting. It's a Baiman...
217:The player who moves before me has a painful expression on her face because the player who moves before her called chase Riichi, and she was unlucky enough to deal into their big hand.
218:Meanwhile, the player who moves after me is grinning widely because their team is now in second place after this win.
219:If Ai hadn't deliberately given them points to even out the point gap between the other three teams, the players from St. Tina's Girls' School might not need to play catch-up so desperately right now.
220:Tsumo, first repeat, 1,400, 2,700.
221:Did you make a mistake? I thought you have a two-way wait. Where did the 40 Fu come from?
222:No, this 4, 5, 6, 6 Man counts as a 4, 5, 6 Mentsu, and the last 6 Man counts as a single-wait Tenpai.
223:Although I feel bad for the team of the player who moves before me because of the situation they are in due to non-match factors, I will still collect the number of points I'm due. At the same time, I won't target the little boy's team. I'll treat my opponents equally.
224:(whisper) If I'm biased towards either side, then I don't really have the right to question Ai's actions...
226:After Nana Shiraishi leaves, Ai looks at me proudly with her legs crossed and smiles.
227:I just told Nana-senpai what I want to do, hehe, and she said she'll support me!
228:She really said that?
229:Before she walked off, Nana-senpai said, "I understand. Don't worry, I'll try my best!" In that case, I can just leave everything to her.
230:She'll try her best, huh...
231:...Well, I think Ai was acting a little strangely in the previous game.
232:I'll feel bad if I keep everything to myself and try to guess her reasons, so I decide to just tell Nana Shiraishi my suspicions.
233:Okay, I'll ask her about this later.
234:Just like that? Don't you think I might be overthinking things?
235:Even if I'm your senior in life, I won't pretend to be better than you in mahjong-related matters. I trust your judgment.
236:Eat something first. The lieutenant battle is almost over, and you need to eat something to have the energy to play against others.
237:Ai happens to enter the room after I finish eating. I don't know when Nana Shiraishi will ask her about the thing we discussed earlier, so I tell her I need to use the toilet and leave the room because I feel a little embarrassed. After that, I wander around outside until it's time to start playing.
238:In the vice-captain battle, the team that moves before me deals into a Baiman by mistake. As a result, the team of children that moves after me ends up climbing to second place. I didn't earn too many points in this Hanchan, but I managed to defend our team's ranking by playing safely and steadily.
239:I see Nana Shiraishi walking towards me after I leave the mahjong room. She gets straight to the point and answers the question I'm most concerned about.
240:Well, I was thinking of a way to ask her about this, but she came to me herself and asked me to go easy on those children. She said she agreed to help them advance to the finals.
241:So she really wasn't playing fairly... What's her reason?
242:I feel a little stupid after asking this question. The reason isn't important anyway, because no reason is an excuse to play unfairly.
243:Ai said those children want the set of toys prize of the finals. She thinks that it's alright to go easy on them since we have so many points.
244:Well... What will you do then?
245:Ah, you're frowning again. Don't you worry, because I'll try my best.
246:If you want to act like a cute junior, you should leave all the difficult things to your senpai. Now be sensible and go to the lounge to cheer for me. Go now.
247:After speaking, Nana Shiraishi turns around and waves with her back to me before disappearing at the other end of the corridor.
248:I reach out and want to ask her what she means when she says she'll try her best, but I don't say anything in the end because I suddenly realize that I don't actually know Nana Shiraishi that well.
249:I'm not confident that she'll give me an answer I can accept...
251:Mmm.
252:I avoid Ai's eyes and sit in front of the screen. Nana Shiraishi also sits down at the mahjong table at the same time, and the game starts.
253:(whisper) She'll do her best, huh...
255:Captain battle, third East round, second repeat
256:A good two-way wait Riichi in the third turn... There are so few clues in this turn that the other players can only play defensively.
257:After two turns, there are still no reliable clues based on Nana Shiraishi's discarded tiles. The person who moves before her wipes some sweat, hesitates for a while, then closes her eyes and plays a 7 Sou.
258:This player has made this reckless move because she wants to achieve something while she is still the dealer. What's more, she might even lose more points than she has earned from her wins so far if Nana Shiraishi manages to Tsumo a Mangan or Haneman. She decided to do this because she didn't really have a choice.
259:She mumbles something, probably a prayer that her tile won't deal into someone's hand. She slowly moves her trembling fingers away from her tile. At the same time, she glances at Nana Shiraishi with uncertainty. When she sees Nana Shiraishi looking at her with a smile, her face suddenly turns pale. It isn't until after Nana Shiraishi continues to draw tiles that she lets out a long sigh of relief...
260:Whoa... Nana-senpai is so mean. I wonder why she deliberately scared that person. I bet that person felt like she had dealt into her hand.
261:She's quite lucky that none of the extra tiles in her hand will deal into the other players' hands. But I think she's given up on winning now. The best outcome would be if she draws a deal-in tile and starts attacking, because then those little children will be able to advance!
262:(whisper) You're making it sound like the player who moves after Nana Shiraishi won't deal into someone's hand at all...
263:The player who moves after Nana seems to really want to win and completely ignores Nana Shiraishi's Riichi by playing one risky tile after another. At the same time, this player happens to have the deal-in tile of 5 Man in their hand, but they probably won't be playing it any time soon because they have 4, 5, 5, 6 Man in their hand.
264:After that, the player who moves before Nana plays two more risky tiles and gets rid of the unnecessary tiles in her hand. I can tell from the look in her eyes that she doesn't care anymore because she's got nothing to lose. Meanwhile, the player who moves after Nana keeps playing with fearless confidence, daring to play any tile just to win.
265:The player sitting opposite Nana, the only one defending normally, looks at the two players sitting on their left and right, then shakes their head and keeps defending. I bet this person is really confused by what's happening.
266:...Ah, she's started to Tenpai.
267:As if she is being rewarded for her bravery, the player who moves before Nana has started to Tenpai, and she also has a two-way wait Tenpai with a 5 and 8 Man, just like Nana Shiraishi. However, she didn't call Riichi. Maybe she is waiting for an even better hand to win more points.
268:Immediately after that, the player who moves after Nana also starts to Tenpai.
269:Riichi!
270:The player who moves after Nana throws a 5 Man tile, which makes a thud as it lands on the table. The player who moves before Nana seizes this opportunity and shouts "Ron" as she pushes down her tiles.
271:Ah, what a shame! But the hand is only worth three Han, so she's nowhere close to making up for her loss from the previous Baiman.
272:Ron.
273:Huh...?
274:The player who moves after Nana stares blankly at her as she reveals her hand. Ai, who is beside me, also makes a look of disbelief.
275:Wh-What's going on?
276:I'm sorry, but this is a double Ron. Let me see... Oh? I have two Ura Dora, which means this is a Haneman.
277:The player who moves after Nana loses three Han to the dealer and a Haneman to a non-dealer player. Factoring in the repeat counter, this player has lost all the points they won from the previous Baiman.
278:After that... no, since the very beginning, Nana Shiraishi has never had the intention to go easy on the child. She has just been playing mahjong like always in this captain battle.
279:After losing the previous assistance they received, the player who moves after Nana makes several mistakes in a row, giving the player who moves before Nana the chance to regain the advantage and ultimately turn the situation around to reach the finals.
280:Haha, so that's what she meant by trying her best. Nana acted the same as usual.
281:I don't understand... This shouldn't have happened...
283:When Nana Shiraishi returns to the lounge, Ai rushes up to her and starts to question her emotionally.
284:Huh? I told you I would try my best, right?
285:Didn't you understand what I was saying? I wanted you to...
286:Nana had no intention of helping you from the beginning. Do you really have to force her to say that out loud?
287:Don't interrupt me while I'm talking to my senpai, you outsider!
288:No, junior here is right. It's necessary to try one's best to win, so how can I go easy on others?
289:...I don't understand. This is just a recreational competition. Why do you have to take it so seriously? ...Even if you want to win, we have way more points than everyone, right?! You didn't need to win with that Haneman!
290:Those children just want that set of toys. Nana-senpai, you always go on about how you lend a helping hand to the weak, so why...
292:If they want that set of toys, I'm happy to give it to them after winning and advancing to the finals, but since we have this rare opportunity to play together at the same table, wouldn't it be a pity if we don't compete with our true skills and enjoy the game together?
293:...I don't think they enjoyed anything. It hasn't been easy for them to get so far. After seeing the chance to get to the finals, do you think they'll really be happy even if you give them that prize in the end?
295:They might feel sad about not making it to the finals, but wouldn't this fuel their desire to become stronger? I would like to give them this prize to encourage them to do better next time instead of giving them a chance to enter the finals out of pity because I think they are weak.
296:No, that's not what I meant...
297:Haha, you don't need to get nervous. I know you don't mean that. What will really make me sad is if you think I helped you in the past because I was pitying you and that I'm an arrogant person who did that for self-satisfaction.
298:Kindness is important, but it's also equally important to take the competition seriously and respect your opponents, right? Okay, that's it for now. Let's prepare for the finals and give that set of toys to those children.
299:Oh wait, there's one more thing. Ai, you should apologize to {var:player_nickname}.
300:Huh?! Why do I have to apologize?
301:Don't be silly. Did you forget what you said? {var:player_nickname} is our teammate and a junior I approve of, not an outsider.
302:I know you were a little emotional earlier, but you should apologize for the rude things you've said.
304:What she said makes sense. Ai, maybe you should just apologize.
306:Since we're all on the same team for this competition, let's just shake hands and make peace.
307:Even you two aren't backing me up?! Geez... f-fine then!
308:Umm... she doesn't need to apologize to me. Let's just forget about this since Nana said it's over.
309:No, that won't do. That's that, and this is this.
310:O-Okay then... (whisper) Just get it over and done with if you're going to apologize...
311:(whisper) Sigh... (loudly) I'M SORRY FOR BEING RUDE EARLIER!
312:Hmm-hmm, very good. That sounds very energetic!
313:It's so energetic that my eardrums are a little sore... Sigh, it really isn't necessary to apologize if you're so unhappy about it.
315:Then what's with that unhappy look on your face?
316:It's because... Nana-senpai said you're a junior she approves of. I... I was here first! I met her first, and I respect her the most, yet she has never said that I'm a junior she approves of. That isn't fair, right?!
317:You're more serious now than during the competition... Hey, don't pull my collar! Nana, this is your fault, so come here and diffuse the situation... Huh? Where is she?
318:She said she doesn't want to get in the way of your youthful exchange, so she has gone to talk to those children about giving them the prize after the competition.
319:Tsk... this woman has run away after sowing a disaster so casually!
320:As a result, I have to endure Ai's sad and resentful gaze and her disgruntled mumbling until the finals start...
321:Will you really give this to us?! Thank you, lady!
322:Hehe, let's play mahjong again next time.
323:Yeah! We'll definitely win next time!
324:After the finals, we exchanged the prize we received for winning the competition with the second runner-up's prize and gave the toy set to the team of children according to our agreement.
325:The two other members need to go, so the only people left here are me, Nana Shiraishi, and Ai.
326:...Those children are so happy, and they look at Nana-senpai like she's a hero.
327:I was worried that they would feel sad about not making it to the finals or even start to hate mahjong because of this. Was I overthinking the situation...?
328:What you were worrying about could have happened too, but even so, I think Nana would stay true to her decision and take all her opponents seriously.
329:Hmph... you win this time.
330:I win? What did I win?
331:I'm not happy to admit this, but I don't understand Nana-senpai well enough right now and couldn't make the same decision as her. That's why you win.
333:...Oh well, I don't want to keep thinking about something this depressing. At least I understand her better today than I did before. That's good enough for me.
334:Just so you know! I'll hear Nana-senpai say that I'm a junior she approves of one day. I won't give up! Anyway... I'm going to go now.
335:Aren't you going to wait for us?
336:No. I want to think carefully about how I should help others in the future...
337:Ai is already gone by the time Nana Shiraishi returns after chatting with the children. After I tell her why Ai left first, Nana Shiraishi places her hands behind her head and smiles.
338:She's so serious about this. In that case, let's not disturb her. Do you have any plans after this?
339:It's getting dark, so let's have dinner.
341:Ah, what a coincidence. There happens to be a sensible junior here.
342:Why are you just standing there then? Let's go.
343:A cup of sweet milk tea with lots of bubbles is the perfect cure for a tired body.
344:Oh, there's something I forgot to say to you.
345:Hmm?
346:You were really cool today, senpai.
347:Hehe, of course!
348:(sobbing) I don't wanna accept...
349:Ai walks up to me with a sullen expression. She says she will apologize to me, but her eyes tell me that she is not convinced.
350:I feel a little complicated after hearing this. I also wondered whether Nana Shiraishi would support Ai earlier and doubted her. Do I really understand Nana Shiraishi better than Ai?
351:That's a great idea! It'll be even better if a certain sensible junior will treat their senpai who has worked hard all day to a cup of bubble tea first.
352:Competition venue lounge
353:After the match
354:Member B
355:Wait a minute. I do like helping people, but I don't think I've said anything like I need to "lend a helping hand to the weak." I'm just an ordinary person, so I'll let real heroes say something that sounds so cool.
362:It's normal for most people to feel a little unhappy after losing a match.
364:She even specially changed the Janto of the Terminals tile. She did all that extra work just to assemble an All Simples with one more Han, making her hand as big as possible to get back at me for making a dig at her.

367:No, Nana-senpai said I have to apologize, so I have to do that. I also think that I was rude earlier, so I should apologize to you.
369:This is what I've been waiting for! Tsumo with a high-Han Haneman!
370:I'm sorry! The line was really long at the bank, which wasted a lot of time. Thanks for waiting so long.
371:Our team was blessed with luck as foretold by Kaavi, and we had a significant lead over the other teams in terms of points by the end of the second round. If we keep up this momentum, maybe we really can win the competition!
372:It's time for the battle between the vice-captains. From the finals onwards, the seating position of the players of participating teams is fixed in each round. That's why the player who moves before me is also from the St. Tina's Girls' School Mahjong Club, and the player who moves after me is a teammate of the little boy who asked Ai for help.
373:Thanks for the hard work guys! Leave the rest to me.
374:Ah, you're back. That wasn't bad. It's a shame you left the room too early just then, so I wasn't able to ask you to coordinate with me. Has Nana-senpai told you about the situation?
375:Riichi.
376:How can Nana-senpai go back on her word?! This isn't what we agreed on!
